\DOC cases_of

\TYPE {cases_of : hol_type -> thm}

\SYNOPSIS
The cases theorem of an inductive type.

\DESCRIBE
A call {cases_of ty} returns the theorem that says that every element of
the type {ty} is one of the constructors of {ty} applied to something.
HOL Light proves that theorem from the induction theorem in its store
(see {cases}); this looks it up by the name of the type, derives it once
and keeps it, because a development that splits on a type a thousand
times should pay for it once.

Where {ty} belongs to a family of types that were defined together,
{cases} gives the theorems of all of them at once and this returns the
conjunct about {ty} itself. A pair has one constructor and no cases
theorem of its own, and {PAIR_SURJECTIVE} is returned for it.

\FAILURE
Fails if {ty} is not a type that was defined by {define_type}, or one of
{num} and {prod}.

\EXAMPLE
{
  # cases_of `:(A)list`;;
  val it : thm = |- forall x. x = [] \/ (exists a0 a1. x = CONS a0 a1)

  # cases_of `:num`;;
  val it : thm = |- forall m. m = 0 \/ (exists n. m = SUC n)
}

\USES
It is what {CASES_ON_TAC} splits a term with.

\SEEALSO
cases, CASES_ON_TAC, distinctness, induction_of, injectivity.

\ENDDOC
